<?xpacket begin="ï»¿" id="W5M0MpCehiHzreSzNTczkc9d"?>
<x:xmpmeta xmlns:x="adobe:ns:meta/">
  <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#">
    <rdf:Description xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" rdf:about="">
      <dc:title>
        <rdf:Alt>
          <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Shattered Trust, Fragile Hope</rdf:li>
        </rdf:Alt>
      </dc:title>
      <dc:description>
        <rdf:Alt>
          <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">&lt;p&gt;A year ago, Catra D'riluth walked out, leaving behind the beautiful home, the life sheâ€™d built, and the woman she loved. Now, every other Friday at 5:30 PM, she stands on the porch for the custody exchange, choking on the hollow echo of the future that shattered. Sheâ€™s just trying to survive the ache of being a visitor in her own past, determined to never let Adora Gray break her heart again.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Adora Gray is drowning in the silence of the house she designed for their family. Sheâ€™s haunted by the ghost of Catraâ€™s laughter and the devastating, unanswered question: How did I not see it?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But the most painful silence is their child Finnâ€™sâ€”a quiet confusion that echoes louder than any argument.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Is the chasm between them too wide to bridge, or can two broken people find a way to stop hurting each otherâ€”and their childâ€”long enough to listen?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;(A story about the architecture of a broken home, the erosion of trust, and the terrifying risk of a second chance.)&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:li>
